#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb0eb10f-8358-5f70-9dcf-5a12623a7ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404101} {:answer/id #uuid "1df643a8-80e1-5748-9179-fabd113dde47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404102} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd75f60-e366-51aa-9704-98e0a480bbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404103} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9540b0-0bdb-50eb-99c1-9744cc3a5660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404104} {:answer/id #uuid "f62ab074-e099-5956-af5c-d32875cc38df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa9540b0-0bdb-50eb-99c1-9744cc3a5660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404104} :answer-field/id #uuid "76990587-1106-5838-a7d3-5abb529f309f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fb53181-29d9-5f89-8598-3c491720e6bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80389" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "639780ae-1398-50d7-b815-e2a195a0e73d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404108} {:answer/id #uuid "c73ad559-0eae-5f15-bdf7-739e613b69ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404109} {:answer/id #uuid "d0038efe-a96a-5281-ad36-c4af7f9c03a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404110} {:answer/id #uuid "c0406221-3155-5859-8236-e58b9f481700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404111} {:answer/id #uuid "d939f8e6-1f46-548c-8adc-229c62fd7d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "639780ae-1398-50d7-b815-e2a195a0e73d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404108} :answer-field/id #uuid "b352245b-eaa4-5fde-aea9-5afac2d00b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84388249-87e9-543c-a81f-fd99783cee24" :question/math-academy-id "q-80392" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15560ba1-c0e7-57a3-bd24-dc19e7e9f52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404115} {:answer/id #uuid "4a752dce-688f-5fd3-becb-69a53449004c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404116} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bff80f-44fe-590e-825f-851b2661193c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404117} {:answer/id #uuid "2042fd4c-3a1d-5b77-a6af-7aa6566dcc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404118} {:answer/id #uuid "8b20c357-a80f-5d68-9f17-47dfa214c55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2042fd4c-3a1d-5b77-a6af-7aa6566dcc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404118} :answer-field/id #uuid "958185d9-bfb3-5c99-9580-566f3546885e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7be4462e-3d0b-5745-b83e-db8e5d74f57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80493" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20692b0c-ecf3-5857-b371-5e00dd088456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404122} {:answer/id #uuid "8297858c-6a75-5e90-8004-887fd5f4fe6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404123} {:answer/id #uuid "21bdb776-d042-5bc3-8def-4d142fb67170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404124} {:answer/id #uuid "9a56eb51-adbb-529e-b66c-37695fb34a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404125} {:answer/id #uuid "3093f458-0d1e-55d2-af6e-2473cd7616a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3093f458-0d1e-55d2-af6e-2473cd7616a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404126} :answer-field/id #uuid "5324a7ce-971d-53cb-b107-19a127a08e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f53e1d4c-a53d-5c3e-8ae8-0e080a8e331f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80496" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$  Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Constants in First-Order Partial Derivative Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37daf35b-b051-5de5-be26-c9ede321ab36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1$, $b=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404129} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb42258-31a2-56c5-aa5a-940f6339e1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1$, $b=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404130} {:answer/id #uuid "92a398de-810f-54f4-a742-9f17711093b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2$, $b=e$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404131} {:answer/id #uuid "36f61f3a-a59d-517f-b778-17ee028f7808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-e$, $b=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404132} {:answer/id #uuid "7d026b63-b9dd-5d7f-972a-f02973c06d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2$, $b=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d026b63-b9dd-5d7f-972a-f02973c06d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2$, $b=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404133} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa7f024f-dcae-5a46-aab4-36686da92a80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f63374b4-82d5-5092-bcef-8ec87e149ab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80766" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y(1+x){e}^{x}+ax{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(bx{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+x){e}^{x}+3ax{y}^{2}=b(1+x){e}^{x}-6x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $(1+x){e}^{x}$ and $x{y}^{2},$ respectively, we see that $b=1$ and $a=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues of a General 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d3a6e5c-6b63-525d-b4d4-f7578f8a390f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {1,-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404136} {:answer/id #uuid "be0e2c11-87fe-59eb-9698-32afe60aafab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-1,2,-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404137} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd9bbcf-ec11-5649-bcb8-ae71202883ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {2,-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404138} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdcb0b4-6e56-57cc-bb23-2e4c99b0b161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-1,-2,3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404139} {:answer/id #uuid "415b6c2c-e8ea-529a-83eb-176e95a19f4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-1,2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bdcb0b4-6e56-57cc-bb23-2e4c99b0b161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-1,-2,3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404139} :answer-field/id #uuid "135ac5a3-acd1-57d7-85af-ac59d7bb8214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba3b9cc6-841d-5636-9777-0bc1e8c24e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80825" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}+6\\lambda +5)-(-1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-7\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $6:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 6\n$$\n\n- Let's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}-7(1)-6 & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Let's pick $x=-1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{3}-7(-1)-6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-1$ is a root of the equation.\n\nSo, we can factor $(\\lambda +1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}-7\\lambda -6$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +1)({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f185774-4d55-515c-9032-8f6832c6999a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Truth Value of a Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ac2392-a6e5-591f-a5a1-17941b5ea7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Possible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404143} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc0e3a4-8b74-5fcf-b175-8552a9860e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Maybe" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404144} {:answer/id #uuid "2b59ca4a-6fb7-5fb3-abb1-7608d84f6622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404145} {:answer/id #uuid "93659c74-5338-5ca4-a3f8-18b1cd2f5221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Sometimes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404146} {:answer/id #uuid "e029fc14-4c44-508f-af5e-dbb44d816ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e029fc14-4c44-508f-af5e-dbb44d816ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404147} :answer-field/id #uuid "5139bddd-0e23-5ba6-baed-c7f7bf6d512d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2aeaa42-0925-5203-ab6b-c6edd9263eda" :question/math-academy-id "q-80999" :question/problem "What is the truth value of the following mathematical statement?\n\n$\\,$ A square has exactly $3$ sides." :question/worked-solution "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false.\n\nThis statement is false because a square has exactly $4$ sides."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d098974e-26ed-5d31-b1cd-a3dbc06e5513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404150} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a92768-6f32-5589-8651-0b25ea1a112b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404151} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd5d749-fe1e-50cd-8a40-2be69a0ab8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404152} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fb75dc-1ab6-5d98-9700-9f4e9f0bef00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404153} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5c324b-f42d-5122-b7c3-fe01bb4516cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd5d749-fe1e-50cd-8a40-2be69a0ab8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404152} :answer-field/id #uuid "235aadc5-963f-5fda-85cc-68d107673c61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "225d5964-b5f6-5f9c-9606-7856be911138" :question/math-academy-id "q-81202" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0+4-2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =4-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64437bc-258f-57d6-8252-86408694ed54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404157} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fce00d-d8dc-5d30-b228-f50bd9f70fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404158} {:answer/id #uuid "ea65c7ad-6c2b-58e5-8658-7ac9d1dfed34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404159} {:answer/id #uuid "06abf95b-dbcf-5d63-a7e2-6b9ef8e25083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404160} {:answer/id #uuid "163ea62e-033a-5464-9c63-6deea7d57755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "163ea62e-033a-5464-9c63-6deea7d57755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404161} :answer-field/id #uuid "630df7f3-d3b0-52b1-a138-2b8c01a3e3ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "811972db-6534-593a-aa1e-100258f3cf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81236" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\nT[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a499ca4-07df-5c8d-b83c-a9910cc2fe6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=v$ and $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404164} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf255f1-3d51-5826-98c1-93901517d605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404165} {:answer/id #uuid "5da5054c-581f-5f31-9886-dad4f588043d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $u={v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404166} {:answer/id #uuid "6060e83f-92e8-5d62-986a-7a4aa49b2792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the lines $u=0$ and $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404167} {:answer/id #uuid "1669bc78-1efd-58f9-91e9-a08a66025cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0$ and on the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1669bc78-1efd-58f9-91e9-a08a66025cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0$ and on the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404168} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd706ed-a6fa-5777-8fba-b9aa9ded06db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82fb995e-103e-58b4-a8e9-4c6b2963c1a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81273" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}\\,\\,v \\\\ u+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}\\,\\,v$ and $y(u,v)=u+{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={u}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2uv & {u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2uv\\cdot 2v-{u}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4u{v}^{2}-{u}^{2} \\\\  & =u(4{v}^{2}-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ u(4{v}^{2}-u) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equation $u(4{v}^{2}-u)=0$ defines a pair of intersecting curves in the $uv$-plane: the line $u=0$ and the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27dd60f7-0060-5908-af95-4c108c3b1718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404171} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2acdfc-59b5-5eb0-aa0e-5c6158877fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $2u+3v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404172} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd5de86-0281-5ef0-9bac-c48c24b2a6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404173} {:answer/id #uuid "90292b65-61da-58e0-bd75-eda1b58d756e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola ${u}^{2}-3{v}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404174} {:answer/id #uuid "22f6e316-1aa7-5c12-abf8-9b28b8e93f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca2acdfc-59b5-5eb0-aa0e-5c6158877fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $2u+3v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404172} :answer-field/id #uuid "e87074f0-9d22-553d-826e-78e5774760d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59e9bc67-c967-5c58-a234-d26582651fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81279" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+3{v}^{2} \\\\ u-2v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+3{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u-2v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 6v \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2)-6v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4u-6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ -4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u+3v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $2u+3v=0$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cbca2a4-5302-5621-923e-847f18587e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A vertical shear with a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404178} {:answer/id #uuid "686c83f8-b2ff-5446-a69b-a6dca4ee631b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A horizontal shear with a shear factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404179} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e87b1e-635e-59bb-a8b0-e23b7a0b0aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404180} {:answer/id #uuid "1c87caa4-7f94-5398-b1e3-967d57492e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404181} {:answer/id #uuid "2280b79f-4cae-53e8-ad6b-44654f7eae72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $y$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c87caa4-7f94-5398-b1e3-967d57492e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404181} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2dd7953-8b90-52cd-be66-71f8f16406ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5883242f-b44f-5ff7-9fa7-30bcd180241a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81324" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of ${R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=\\frac{1}{2}i$ and $T(j)=j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $i$ and  $j.$\n\n![](images/34/34c82826da26290fc890671734c354fbfd9c253f1cfaca38de1ea615434e6766.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=\\frac{1}{2}i$ and $T(j)=j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](images/02/02470083dea4808af1854e9ed88ab496828a429c593af780209d8a6d4b15a6b1.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9af17e52-688b-5b75-8902-65be7464d50b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Given Vectors Belong to the Kernel of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5009b51a-3311-5349-a0d4-9263093c408c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404185} {:answer/id #uuid "15868a01-ce63-5e6b-b375-fec48a6e6ad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404186} {:answer/id #uuid "55de43a8-0397-50c5-867b-d34348940323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404187} {:answer/id #uuid "5c165536-c143-5156-8063-ecfdc1329b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404188} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1de765-7e1f-51a4-a847-e7a575a67eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5009b51a-3311-5349-a0d4-9263093c408c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404185} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e82b63e-4a76-52fe-b5a0-9529b5e4e1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fe978ee-e242-5740-b3cc-178c387af89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81386" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+4y \\\\ y+z \\\\ x-4z\\end{aligned}]$ and the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the vectors that belong to the kernel of $T$ are ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd566f7-15e2-59d9-bf6a-1fa3598399b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Composite Function Using a Standard Maclaurin Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f99b133-4a61-5da9-8229-2d15c1976133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{720}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404192} {:answer/id #uuid "9040cf5a-c20f-5c3d-8188-6973a3292336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{240}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404193} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc4cf98-68bd-567f-b621-955fa5da11dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{240}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404194} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbcf19d-8dfb-53a4-baf5-a9db5210ac2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{720}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404195} {:answer/id #uuid "4c08336c-5cbd-5687-910d-5e791a42e28f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{240}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f99b133-4a61-5da9-8229-2d15c1976133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}=1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{720}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404192} :answer-field/id #uuid "48887a96-5fe7-5b45-98ea-8163f74623cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0508a683-ff80-5adf-84b7-14d7f45257c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-8171" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $\\sqrt{x}$ in the above expansion and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x} & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{4}}{4!}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{6}}{6!}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{720}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bc0be03-9369-508e-9765-8c88871ec20b" :knowledge-point/title "The Range of a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b7885b-ae95-575e-97f6-8a71e1b57ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404199} {:answer/id #uuid "65627c21-66cb-5f02-8ba6-0c37010c9bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404200} {:answer/id #uuid "dea138f5-3204-5f95-9262-ff365ea22b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404201} {:answer/id #uuid "3f211c15-8d28-53f3-8403-b6b4d9ef7e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no maximum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404202} {:answer/id #uuid "21d62bc4-f68d-55ab-b0ca-9dee218d1167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f211c15-8d28-53f3-8403-b6b4d9ef7e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no maximum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404202} :answer-field/id #uuid "64bfa454-0c70-5d48-80bc-d4490bd1867f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b338b5cf-801c-50c4-9622-d9ab0fa4da90" :question/math-academy-id "q-81863" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-3)+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no maximum value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58ec96-53b3-5317-9801-c730728f32c6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Contrapositive of a Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59ef73e-2be3-53b3-ad02-9dc50f3be05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is not warm outside, then you don't need a jacket." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404205} {:answer/id #uuid "066a4a63-27d3-54e5-b1e8-2493f211b5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you need a jacket, then it is warm outside." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404206} {:answer/id #uuid "55062177-0b75-5d06-a6a5-cdf3aa66a762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't need a jacket, then it is warm outside." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404207} {:answer/id #uuid "25b3e2ab-6ad3-5e7c-a3e4-9a4c09ebf969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is warm outside, then you don't need a jacket." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404208} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8aa02d-eb4a-599b-b592-da512b965916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you need a jacket, then it is not warm outside." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55062177-0b75-5d06-a6a5-cdf3aa66a762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't need a jacket, then it is warm outside." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404207} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d552753-26be-5a35-820e-a8e846bf8657" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "929e0321-87ad-5ccc-a7b5-9aeb909c70ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-81985" :question/problem "Find the contrapositive of the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ **If** it is not warm outside, **then** you need a jacket." :question/worked-solution "In our conditional statement: \n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ It is not warm outside.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ You need a jacket.\n\nThe contrapositive of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\n¬B⇒¬A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negations of $A$ and $B:$\n\n- $¬A:$ It is warm outside.\n\n- $¬B:$ You don't need a jacket.\n\nTherefore, the contrapositive is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If you don't need a jacket, then it is warm outside."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec5acb94-b33a-5921-b761-6f78db3f25f7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Converse of a Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7d619e0-9c27-57b7-88a2-a8566007c2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is not winter, then it is not snowing." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404212} {:answer/id #uuid "4327cf36-0d54-568f-9b23-fb28f90fac91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is snowing, then it is winter." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404213} {:answer/id #uuid "63c8f492-71bb-5a22-aa16-d6742bb59a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is winter, then it is not snowing." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404214} {:answer/id #uuid "3b39a0d4-fe13-5d8d-8219-7578a6fcf9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is not snowing, then it is winter." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404215} {:answer/id #uuid "72312945-3764-584f-9205-240825178c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is winter, then it is snowing." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7d619e0-9c27-57b7-88a2-a8566007c2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If it is not winter, then it is not snowing." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404212} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b467a52-98b6-5550-9876-540b62223358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "370105fd-4191-5aeb-88c5-e95f00d93193" :question/math-academy-id "q-82627" :question/problem "Find the converse of the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ **If** it is not snowing, **then** it is not winter." :question/worked-solution "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ It is not snowing.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ It is not winter.\n\nThe converse of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\nB⇒A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the converse is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If it is not winter, then it is not snowing."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec5acb94-b33a-5921-b761-6f78db3f25f7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Converse of a Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23228fce-70f0-5dba-9000-708acac135b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are watching TV, then you are listening to music." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404219} {:answer/id #uuid "5857c6c1-9129-5867-b73b-a4a5c5d0d9cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are listening to music, then you are not watching TV." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404220} {:answer/id #uuid "9063fc79-ede2-57e3-a9f9-32c94704548c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are listening to music, then you are watching TV." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404221} {:answer/id #uuid "59e3af6f-b80c-5ecf-960d-802915fd17be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are not listening to music, then you are watching TV." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404222} {:answer/id #uuid "c78db225-a5cc-51a0-a9b1-3f7a16c6e468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are watching TV, then you are not listening to music." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5857c6c1-9129-5867-b73b-a4a5c5d0d9cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you are listening to music, then you are not watching TV." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404220} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e370986-ac15-5412-8ce9-6f960a44aba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89a8d37a-6c72-523d-8fc0-d006e8d4daf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-82630" :question/problem "Find the converse of the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ **If** you are not watching TV, **then** you are listening to music." :question/worked-solution "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ You are not watching TV.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ You are listening to music.\n\nThe converse of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\nB⇒A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the converse is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If you are listening to music, then you are not watching TV."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "857d696d-6a3f-5fff-a5e1-b05148f28f55" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Sentences Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25b3b537-e2d4-5af5-abe1-7fe27d620072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is blue." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404225} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8926ca-6ebf-5aa1-80b8-915743a3f1a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is red." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404226} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1aeba2-f7a1-5ed9-8109-e70dd146aca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is blue or red." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404227} {:answer/id #uuid "2b83dfed-9c07-56fa-b16a-12c95687af8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is blue and red." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404228} {:answer/id #uuid "924f8062-92c3-57c2-a169-650f347777d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is blue or red but not both." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f1aeba2-f7a1-5ed9-8109-e70dd146aca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The pen is blue or red." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404227} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca5578dd-0a57-5cc2-809c-152390f18874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ae3ad46-5289-52fe-9fde-b40fc7aa7003" :question/math-academy-id "q-82720" :question/problem "Simplify the following sentence: \"The pen is blue and it is a blue pen, or the pen is blue or it is a red pen.\"" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the phrase \"the pen is blue and it is a blue pen\" to just \"the pen is blue.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The pen is blue, or the pen is blue or it is a red pen.\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma in the sentence:\n\n\"The pen is blue or the pen is blue, or it is a red pen.\"\n\nNow, we simplify the phrase \"the pen is blue or the pen is blue\" to just \"the pen is blue.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The pen is blue, or it is a red pen.\"\n\nFinally, we simplify the sentence to\n\n\"The pen is blue or red.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149709f4-f9d8-529f-87af-a022ec1d46a8" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Sentences Using the Commutative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b32a31f4-569d-51f0-9177-a1505090b425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is new but not red" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404232} {:answer/id #uuid "03d7b53f-1cda-5f14-aa6d-87acf47842ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is red but not new" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404233} {:answer/id #uuid "a997887d-6b8a-5033-a7c7-39403e21d00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is new and red" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404234} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0f91ed-949b-5609-8a80-2ea003d3abbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is red" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404235} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4804d0-20da-5a86-b01a-abf2739587c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is new or red" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a997887d-6b8a-5033-a7c7-39403e21d00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The cup is new and red" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404234} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1094ea0-4165-5ef2-8aaf-f67fca2d0314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edbfe110-6f37-5a51-8f96-227364b973f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83001" :question/problem "Identify the sentence that is equivalent to the following: \"The cup is red and it is new.\"" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"The cup is red and new.\"\n\nUsing the commutative law, we can swap the order of the words \"red\" and \"new.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"The cup is new and red.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa22294c-97df-59d3-a2ef-bb723379bd93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404239} {:answer/id #uuid "05c76659-9ee2-5ea1-9921-4c61064bc47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2)/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404240} {:answer/id #uuid "d957c339-7d86-5b49-bfb4-f34772448576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-1)/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404241} {:answer/id #uuid "59acccda-8ad0-5671-8d3b-1eba77285637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404242} {:answer/id #uuid "08886f8e-1110-502c-b6b0-62359e9dd0d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4)/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08886f8e-1110-502c-b6b0-62359e9dd0d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4)/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404243} :answer-field/id #uuid "5399864b-fdb6-560a-bb1c-eadfa2e8485b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "177e97c5-b0fe-5fdf-abe1-80b42d3faf53" :question/math-academy-id "q-8306" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2yx+y{x}^{2}$ that satisfies $y(1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2yx+y{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(2x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=1$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =1 \\\\ K{e}^{{1}^{2}+{1}^{3}/3} & =1 \\\\ K{e}^{4/3} & =1 \\\\ K & ={e}^{-4/3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2yx+y{x}^{2}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4/3}\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3}={e}^{({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4)/3}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01db7ff6-6460-5d8d-ade8-71a8e02c2a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{19}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404246} {:answer/id #uuid "d8373422-756d-5371-80d9-489591080546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{17}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404247} {:answer/id #uuid "635cb5d7-7f18-5bb2-a15b-2282c9accf79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{8}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404248} {:answer/id #uuid "4da8621d-de59-5cf2-8a08-783a60173651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404249} {:answer/id #uuid "064bd6fd-ada7-5cba-9a3e-93099a992f58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01db7ff6-6460-5d8d-ade8-71a8e02c2a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{8}^{-1/2}≈\\frac{19}{54}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404246} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f9d6d4-3621-5ba8-8abe-069970462f3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f750b509-3c63-5fc0-8993-9bde234b6a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83217" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{-1/2}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of ${8}^{-1/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ f(9) & ={9}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(9)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1/2} & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 27}(x-9)+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x-9}{54}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=8$ into the above to approximate ${8}^{-1/2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{-1/2} & ≈\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{8-9}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{54}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de71fcb6-36ca-581a-92ea-47c1d0c6bf10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Product of Functions Using Standard Maclaurin Expansions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6684cb3-66f7-5670-a618-240321438de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{120}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404253} {:answer/id #uuid "d8eb2141-deaf-5745-8f39-c0df69c4795f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{12}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{60}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404254} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f058db-10d3-5127-a407-810a87d6f495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404255} {:answer/id #uuid "6537e3c8-b8ab-55e5-9855-26f836f4373a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{24}+\\frac{{x}^{9}}{96}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404256} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccc3d10-6b2b-5fed-b615-462c22afdb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{120}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ccc3d10-6b2b-5fed-b615-462c22afdb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{120}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404257} :answer-field/id #uuid "38f38032-483e-5ebe-a2ba-4fc05febd614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83cee5be-d033-528b-aa1a-c2f13225ee96" :question/math-academy-id "q-83429" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an expansion for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we multiply this series by ${x}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ={x}^{2}(x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{120}-⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cccc9be8-40ad-59d1-85aa-0c32e8379a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-10$ and ${f}_{max}=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404260} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57171d-50ae-555b-9220-9f9b5b798b9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404261} {:answer/id #uuid "097476c4-4c40-568d-90a8-2be9e9dfcb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404262} {:answer/id #uuid "ae56b583-e6d7-5084-af2a-bd8d9b194ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404263} {:answer/id #uuid "d5491fbd-ca0b-57cf-bb3e-94617777e540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5491fbd-ca0b-57cf-bb3e-94617777e540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404264} :answer-field/id #uuid "24e0c5a8-3acd-57db-aba7-34b3ec30ab80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74b4ac53-7bdb-5efd-8618-b21e911d16f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83479" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8ce943f8a77fe5756fdaf77185d8b59493ef0da95da3605f1f2416786f9ec394.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=xy-4$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y,\\,x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0,\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,4)$ and $(2,4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4) \\\\  & =(t)(4)-4 \\\\  & =4t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,4)$ and $(2,4)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =(t)({t}^{2})-4 \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}-4)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution $t=0$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one candidate point, $(0,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,4),\\,(-2,4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(-2,4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,4)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $4$ at the points $(2,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d23ea584-4a5e-5d3f-b114-caef1a56d6ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404267} {:answer/id #uuid "178d8403-eb54-593d-abc3-5b52a35b7885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404268} {:answer/id #uuid "ee26e4a3-b2b4-56a4-a778-0012ee7e2935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404269} {:answer/id #uuid "38e4fc3c-40eb-5ddc-a361-e415766dbedb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404270} {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404271} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d75aab-799f-5138-9f8f-42b050f51ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef24453f-f045-5d0a-8ed3-0ca5b930bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-8360" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=0\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5069fb02-022e-544e-b7ca-c45166ca4bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404274} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcfa690-e3cd-5cc8-9177-2dc5f0ed3023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404275} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec33d3c-cfb8-5e66-a78e-a3afbcab3a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404276} {:answer/id #uuid "2b98ed15-9816-5e8c-8df1-651d5c3a7b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404277} {:answer/id #uuid "e5065de5-7957-55f6-bf19-b00b1fba3806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{y-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ec33d3c-cfb8-5e66-a78e-a3afbcab3a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404276} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19317f0-552c-5bb1-aa73-1ed888b17d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29260781-8760-5b93-aff1-74357dc9fb2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8369" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}-(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}+(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8e2916-1175-544e-9d6d-7e8a9cd2eca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404281} {:answer/id #uuid "812a013d-6c97-5c8d-8555-41c27f26371f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404282} {:answer/id #uuid "62777a5a-2c5b-5c2c-bc49-88fd1f72656d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404283} {:answer/id #uuid "58ff7c16-1b9d-5ada-98a5-763a6cfe2824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404284} {:answer/id #uuid "1184eb62-ac2c-5e6b-abd1-f1c4d51d39b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58ff7c16-1b9d-5ada-98a5-763a6cfe2824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8de3def-44e0-5291-9c5d-3c0a5037be6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c42e4814-ec50-5416-8161-bbb51b0ed62b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83710" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91c8360c-3a50-5dde-b1bc-982c3982cc9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404288} {:answer/id #uuid "f6699882-8461-5d24-9c25-bf00717bab1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404289} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b4c43c-1866-559f-80a2-eb60c22ddff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404290} {:answer/id #uuid "77ed7e08-d0f9-52c5-bb87-4330aef31eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404291} {:answer/id #uuid "4129fe51-c35a-5376-82e4-222db49da083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9b4c43c-1866-559f-80a2-eb60c22ddff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404290} :answer-field/id #uuid "e147ba3d-c970-55a9-ac96-8187378e9cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "303c15f5-3e83-55c0-a519-b60eaa51efba" :question/math-academy-id "q-83854" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9c721ea-d96e-5bf0-bc9c-8798a2a4ddf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404295} {:answer/id #uuid "4f82210a-e608-50a1-bf42-9e7411d614b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404296} {:answer/id #uuid "94c87bfa-134c-55bd-87ae-8cfa1aedb050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404297} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e1ec20-80aa-55b5-9af6-1c4b410f8f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404298} {:answer/id #uuid "606da9ae-0a33-5676-904e-bbed5b045212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c721ea-d96e-5bf0-bc9c-8798a2a4ddf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404295} :answer-field/id #uuid "99303798-a587-5408-b480-605526c75a44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "497d051f-d1fb-59f1-b865-99c679ab4f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-83860" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c249cf3a-7864-5132-80fa-c18a3ea915c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercepts of a Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dd6f7f5-37b3-532d-8604-ed7c9c933ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404302} {:answer/id #uuid "27d07b8f-b1e9-5fce-8842-2396695c113b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404303} {:answer/id #uuid "0328b079-91e5-5b86-b53f-6e81abca1363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404304} {:answer/id #uuid "6217ec90-1f6d-59be-8656-6ad6c5565558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404305} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0207ca-8734-51b5-98fe-ceb135d74c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no $y$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0207ca-8734-51b5-98fe-ceb135d74c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no $y$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404306} :answer-field/id #uuid "387fbfdb-9f04-5077-9421-33470d17faf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1131433c-1254-58aa-b6db-67a84792b7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-84046" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $9{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ 9(0)^{2}-{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ -{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions.\nSo, there are no $y$-intercepts."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c249cf3a-7864-5132-80fa-c18a3ea915c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercepts of a Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35b03bdf-0e6c-570c-a516-bba9f81cd6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404309} {:answer/id #uuid "90d6b647-06d9-5006-80fb-4f3c7d8c4b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2)$ and $(0,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404310} {:answer/id #uuid "38c85c74-56cb-53b6-99b2-2ff6bfc5b6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404311} {:answer/id #uuid "4361a63d-c6aa-5054-981d-0dbdb3ae3845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\pm 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404312} {:answer/id #uuid "63643cf4-ce96-571d-b4c0-9f8c8508a137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no $y$-intercepts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35b03bdf-0e6c-570c-a516-bba9f81cd6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404309} :answer-field/id #uuid "b803308c-82e6-5338-8f67-691fc6a2b7e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3982bc28-35e9-548b-a313-78e4ed431710" :question/math-academy-id "q-84048" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ (0-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 9-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ y & =-6,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,-6)$ and $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e82133f4-cf40-52df-89fc-b9095a2ed393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=13$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404316} {:answer/id #uuid "243d60de-8290-5d22-a02c-f2dd575ecea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404317} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2cbf42-1425-51b3-9612-2bbf89e799f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404318} {:answer/id #uuid "f5be6a6c-ff71-53df-b6a3-b84c12d5df62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404319} {:answer/id #uuid "896a5cf1-06e8-57d7-a9d3-ce00f8cd43ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f2cbf42-1425-51b3-9612-2bbf89e799f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404318} :answer-field/id #uuid "8345e678-341d-5cbb-a53f-7923d2b2c4f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca9903e3-49ec-5aad-b531-9a0f3deac3f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84592" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ a & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ a & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & b\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cba355-0ee9-5493-b5b4-b01907ba7853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404323} {:answer/id #uuid "88bc01f1-e620-5d5f-b135-3c829509a914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404324} {:answer/id #uuid "c44c5852-2bff-553c-b41d-6c60dda93e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404325} {:answer/id #uuid "e6647b56-89a1-5a81-85bf-14ccfa47147b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404326} {:answer/id #uuid "814df066-a51f-5318-8a89-ed554769eecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6647b56-89a1-5a81-85bf-14ccfa47147b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404326} :answer-field/id #uuid "9151fede-a06c-511f-acdd-16c15e3d7d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37e87bc7-80c8-5654-8c99-e96b9d9f6c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-85640" :question/problem "![](images/97/97c98411b3455c78c9e061cf54fa76eabfccc369e9e364a5366eb58bd5d2935d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7},$ what is the value of $cot⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/8c/8c7889d63cf5894236bc5f5e24feb64ecf5a47acb2c07c807a4e65458a991232.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is negative in the fourth quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{7})}{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bce3c75-6a0d-53d7-9a0d-6b6d8acd31bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404330} {:answer/id #uuid "2613efdb-cd4c-5ddd-8173-e348bdcb96f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404331} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7b0ef2-5302-5b29-89fc-f8f26371b54c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404332} {:answer/id #uuid "39dd9d4a-1c32-59d2-b731-ba230d302e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404333} {:answer/id #uuid "b99f4a1b-d16f-5aa6-a399-a33703bd010f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bce3c75-6a0d-53d7-9a0d-6b6d8acd31bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404330} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48430d0-09a9-535a-843b-f4fdfbee498f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0902cd66-9d18-50eb-a894-3b1edfc4b17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-85643" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3be6fa94deb7fc38d870e5a31cc81c3cfd6b9ab84c21340e7e88f7246b1058bc.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{7},$ what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/17/17e6320115f2cf8b5ffad6abc099169774536af20d2559b453aaa97c1286d96d.png)\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{24}}{7} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{6}}=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b68c25b-c193-5a40-829b-fd51b04788c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404337} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd1cdc0-3122-5e73-85f9-af559fd24792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404338} {:answer/id #uuid "7c200ff8-dd75-5421-837d-9ef23ff5522e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404339} {:answer/id #uuid "5671ea71-84dd-554c-92ae-a5caaefc57b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404340} {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34743567-dc8f-5027-8a31-2da4c77b759c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=12,\\,w=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404341} :answer-field/id #uuid "3733f599-455f-5b42-9817-59fac3c5944c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7895edb1-f2c2-5302-ad9e-dc0b6ea9c033" :question/math-academy-id "q-86413" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}-4w+11=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}-4w+11) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}+4w-11 & =0 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}+w) & =11 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}w+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =11 \\\\ 4[{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1 & =11 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =11+1 \\\\ 4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=12.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{12}{4} \\\\ {(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |w+\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ w+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ w & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a96b493-4dc4-5e3d-affb-5104b074be81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404344} {:answer/id #uuid "a2847f48-b197-5c55-ac63-4e530e4e9ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3d04fe-ed8a-51ec-a836-0b919705ce38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404346} {:answer/id #uuid "93a6ab5a-4ea6-5f88-bef2-511b7baac0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404347} {:answer/id #uuid "f625d56d-5576-52e1-a8c2-d2b4aa1f2141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2847f48-b197-5c55-ac63-4e530e4e9ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3a12275-0df5-56dd-8578-50b10f58f3d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e1734459-a432-53d0-859c-83686d8f7d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-86414" :question/problem "The equation $-3{x}^{2}+36x-94=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}+36x-94) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-36x+94 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-36x+94 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-36x & =-94 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-12x] & =-94 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}] & =-94 \\\\ 3[{(x-6)}^{2}-36] & =-94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x-6)}^{2}-3\\cdot 36 & =-94 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2}-108 & =-94 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2} & =-94+108 \\\\ 3{(x-6)}^{2} & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-6$, $q=14.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=14-(-6)=14+6=20.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404351} {:answer/id #uuid "fff341dd-b41a-5304-ad09-cc4af765adfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404352} {:answer/id #uuid "0b80197a-b970-5c44-9c91-75cf359e8575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=5$ and $w=\\frac{11}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404353} {:answer/id #uuid "fe959228-e459-5b19-9cf7-7d8a7111d3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404354} {:answer/id #uuid "89145d06-76ec-5ca5-93f9-df5b33533d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $w=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404351} :answer-field/id #uuid "417df0a0-d7e8-5502-826d-e0777e84a95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "211d779e-92bf-5786-beb2-56b9bec0ebf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86415" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}+40w-75=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}+40w-75) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w & =-75 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}-10w) & =-75 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}-2\\cdot 5w+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}] & =-75 \\\\ 4[{(w-5)}^{2}-25] & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2}-4\\cdot 25 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2}-100 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =-75+100 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=25.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w-5)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w-5)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w-5| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w-5 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =5\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =5+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ w & =5-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead01c60-0b8e-5265-95fa-6b4cdaec742d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404358} {:answer/id #uuid "db0835b3-9555-5f10-b783-ce2279912fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404359} {:answer/id #uuid "c48ddddc-9d40-5c48-8b18-1935496f2905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404360} {:answer/id #uuid "23d9111e-7ce1-561b-9f4f-29ebc2c46ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404361} {:answer/id #uuid "31a2fcdf-48af-528a-bc62-4f070d949ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and ${f}_{max}=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c48ddddc-9d40-5c48-8b18-1935496f2905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-7$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404360} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c6b9bc-2cad-5aef-8fbc-0df5f811dfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32f5ebff-a024-5f63-b943-142f3a504252" :question/math-academy-id "q-86709" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+6x-3$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-4.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,-3),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,-4),$ $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ and $(2,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/96/96096b5f8944785f1af1bbc4fd4942e081b4b3999887c0fa8d5d76008af454d5.png)\n\nSince the point $(0,-3)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,-4),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-3) & =2(0)(-3)+6(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(-2,-4) & =2(-2)(-4)+6(-2)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-1,-1) & =2(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)+6(1)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(2,-4) & =2(2)(-4)+6(2)-3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,-4),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c6ddfc8-d3e9-5bef-b036-cd7895639820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404365} {:answer/id #uuid "c9416bc5-9684-5819-b799-c1e0f49a858d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-6$ and ${f}_{max}=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404366} {:answer/id #uuid "36a97ef2-1a82-57a0-968f-54bc87d7f1d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404367} {:answer/id #uuid "d805fe20-95dc-51e9-a39d-77b1b85929f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404368} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9268c4-7438-538b-8f4d-d2e4cbcf3323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-6$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36a97ef2-1a82-57a0-968f-54bc87d7f1d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-12$ and ${f}_{max}=12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404367} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd6de7a6-023f-5e00-9b0c-d361277d5ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25973738-10c4-5a96-a292-a5b1bfa48a02" :question/math-academy-id "q-86712" :question/problem "![](images/20/20f279f5936987374965cbba01e7e089d88dae1c62767b1ab8eb9ebaea0e5ce9.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=6x-2xy$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-{x}^{2}/2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}6-2y,\\,-2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6-2y=0 \\\\ -2x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,3).$ Since the point $(0,3)$ does not belong to the closed region of $D,$ we do not include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(0) \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}+2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation has no solution inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$ So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain two candidates:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-12$ | $12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $12$ at the point $(2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233ac657-1b6c-5153-ae47-c73f6a18839b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404372} {:answer/id #uuid "1398af4c-7ac7-570a-a8f9-3e7b4fb50230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404373} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7069d6-ca3e-5408-8724-9f786c398052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404374} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9e5045-1ffc-5c1d-8753-b170299e5ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404375} {:answer/id #uuid "015fd256-8e43-5c2a-9c03-aa641f0627f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "233ac657-1b6c-5153-ae47-c73f6a18839b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404372} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a29c76-a92d-5a15-9982-689804fc2dba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8392959c-a393-5152-9307-9f96b5859277" :question/math-academy-id "q-86722" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-4xy$ on the closed elliptical region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 9}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4y,\\,-4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4y=0 \\\\ -4x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-8$ | $10$ | $-8$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the points $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}).$"}]}
